  reason: "The one producer arm for std.compiler_entry NativeServeDriver services (gunbc.target_binding NativeServeProgramProducer { entry, probe }), the instrument the operator ruling of 2026-10-03 asked for beside the arm. Its host part is only what no .dag fold can do: emit and build through the existing prepare_emitted_compiler_for_entry, spawn the built service, read the address it announces, write the requests gunbc.native_serve_probe native_serve_probe_requests names to real TCP connections, and carry the bytes that came back out unjudged, plus one launch with gunbc.native_serve_probe native_serve_probe_refused_revision whose status and stderr it carries the same way. Every verdict -- the cases, what each owes, the join of responses to cases, the refused start -- is native_serve_probe_standing, evaluated by the host and mapped through cli_run classify_exit, so the host adds none. It is ONE arm generic over its entry AND over the row's probe (gunbc.native_serve_probe_plan NativeServeProbe): the reader module and the declared phase set (peer instance, refused launch, stuck-ceiling exit) are fields of the binding row, read by read_native_serve_binding through gunbc.instrument_targets and handed to the run as NativeServePhases, so the host branches on no binding and a phase a row does not declare is not run; a row declaring a phase its reader cannot judge, or a reader judging a phase the row does not declare, is refused before anything is built (gunbc.native_serve_probe_plan native_serve_phase_refusals). The fixture probe is re-expressed through the same fields with all three phases declared, so there is one path; the approval broker's native service and the device-crypto verifier service (gunbc.auth.approval_device_crypto_serve, declaring no phase) are binding rows, not other arms. THE PURPOSE TEST (DESIGN section 3, v1 maintenance standing): this growth serves the v2 self-host program, because the verifier service it makes runnable is the emitted .dag folds (extdeps.crypto.signature verify_signature, extdeps.apple.app_attest verify_attestation and verify_assertion) that the native broker will link in process, and it adds no v1 semantics. It also starts a SECOND instance of the same entry as the local server its bound REST handler is pointed at (native broker 2N), reads that instance's announced port, and asks native_serve_probe_requests for the requests with that port as the argument, so the request bytes are still the reader's. STARTUP FOLD (v2 self-host purpose, no v1 semantics change): emit_native_serve_driver_main_rs gains one OPTIONAL step, selected by native_serve_declares_startup (a driver whose pipeline module declares no gunbc.native_serve native_serve_startup_entry_function is emitted exactly as before): the rendered main reads its own executable's octets, hands them to the entry's native_serve_startup fold BEFORE binding, and on gunbc.native_serve NativeServeStartupRefused prints the typed refusal line and exits gunbc.native_serve native_serve_startup_refused_exit_code without binding. The host part is only the read of the executable bytes; every verdict (obligations judged, the digest, the receipt) is the .dag fold, so the device-crypto verifier service can refuse to start unless gunbc.auth.approval_device_crypto_realization device_crypto_obligations all hold for the binary it is. Controls: //gunbc/instruments:native-serve-startup-refusal (a refusing startup blocks bind) and //gunbc/instruments:native-serve (no startup fold, unchanged). It waits, bounded, for the subject to exit by itself past the stuck-worker ceiling: process control and byte carriage, no decision.",

  owning_dissolution_lane: "v1-hand-queue-drain" as RoadmapNodeId,

